WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:05.450
This is a Wendover Productions video made
possible by Squarespace—get your domain

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:07.750
before its gone with Squarespace.

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:12.570
Moving the president of the United States
from one location to another is a logistical

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:16.520
nightmare that happens every single day.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:20.360
The American president is one of the most
powerful persons in the world and there is

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:24.490
no time when the president is more exposed
than when they’re in transport.

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:28.700
Two of the four successful assassinations
of American presidents were when they were

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:30.860
traveling—Garfield and Kennedy.

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:35.270
For that reason, the secret service devotes
an enormous amount of resources to ensuring

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:39.000
the president gets from one place to another
safely.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.410
The presidential motorcade is perhaps the
most impressive method of transportation.

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:47.990
It includes up to 40 or 50 vehicles and over
100 people.

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:50.829
Let’s take a look at some footage of a motorcade.

00:00:50.829 --> 00:00:53.730
This is the lead car, or in this case, motorcycle.

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:57.510
As the name suggests, it’s job is to lead
the motorcade, but it’s not actually the

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:58.510
first vehicle.

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:02.530
What we don’t see here is the route car
which would’ve come through about five minutes

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:04.160
before the lead car.

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:09.000
The route car is always followed by about
20-30 motorcycles which play the essential

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.990
role of clearing the route of cars.

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:16.100
When driving on a highway, they’ll speed
ahead and close exits so that no cars can

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:20.250
come on the highway, although they have to
use a different technique in cities.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:24.790
When driving in DC, where other drivers are
used to frequent motorcades, the motorcycles

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:30.110
often drive in a “V” pattern pushing cars
to the side to clear the way for the motorcade.

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:33.900
This leads to a disruption of only two or
three minutes.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:38.790
Outside of DC, drivers are less used to motorcades
so the motorcycles will typically completely

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:43.700
block off the intersecting streets to create
one continuous clear path for the president.

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:48.310
The entire first section of the motorcade
isn’t actually run by the secret service—its

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:51.460
run by the local police jurisdiction of wherever
the president is.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:56.080
The fewer secret service cars and individuals
needed for motorcades the better since both

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:00.760
the cars and agents need to travel to wherever
the president is visiting in advance which

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.380
is, of course, incredibly expensive.

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:06.500
The next part of the motorcade is what’s
known as the secure package.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:10.499
It’s the essential core to the motorcade
because its the group of cars that directly

00:02:10.499 --> 00:02:11.980
secures the president.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:15.900
In case of attack, this group of cars can
detach from the rest of the motorcade and

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:17.870
function as an independent unit.

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:21.840
The most important car in the secure package
is the president’s limo but one can never

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:25.609
be sure which one that is because there are
always two limos.

00:02:25.609 --> 00:02:29.160
These two limos are identical—they even
have the same license plate.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:33.160
That way an attacker can’t, for example,
have a spotter tell them which car the president

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:34.169
left their origin in.

00:02:34.169 --> 00:02:37.169
The limos themselves are no ordinary limos.

00:02:37.169 --> 00:02:43.139
They weigh between 15,000 and 20,000 pounds
and are built on a modified truck bed.

00:02:43.139 --> 00:02:47.609
The entire car is bulletproof including 5
inch thick bulletproof windows.

00:02:47.609 --> 00:02:52.329
The only window that can open is the drivers
window, reportedly so they can pay tolls.

00:02:52.329 --> 00:02:57.739
The entire car is hermetically sealed to protect
the president from a chemical attack and onboard

00:02:57.739 --> 00:03:03.639
are rocket powered grenades, night vision
optics, a tear gas cannon, pump-action shotguns,

00:03:03.639 --> 00:03:07.049
bottled oxygen, and two pints of the president’s
blood type.

00:03:07.049 --> 00:03:12.209
This car, leading the secure package, is the
United States Secret Service Electronic Countermeasures

00:03:12.209 --> 00:03:13.209
Suburban.

00:03:13.209 --> 00:03:18.239
It leads the secure package because its purpose
is to jam any remote activated explosives

00:03:18.239 --> 00:03:19.430
along the route.

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:24.159
These two tall antennas do that by sending
out a huge range of radio frequencies that

00:03:24.159 --> 00:03:26.689
would block any bomb activation signal.

00:03:26.689 --> 00:03:31.559
The two domes on top of the car are most likely
what’s known as electronic warfare sensors.

00:03:31.559 --> 00:03:35.760
They’re essentially radars that can detect
when a rocket propelled grenade or anti-tank

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:37.430
guided missile is launched.

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:41.549
When it detects a launch, the car would most
likely activate a number of smoke grenades

00:03:41.549 --> 00:03:46.129
spread out throughout the secure package which
would help throw off the grenade or missile,

00:03:46.129 --> 00:03:49.279
but of course this is all classified so that’s
speculation.

00:03:49.279 --> 00:03:51.109
This car is the control car.

00:03:51.109 --> 00:03:55.489
It carries the most essential staff like the
president’s military aide and doctor.

00:03:55.489 --> 00:03:59.439
In the event of an attack, they’re both
essential to the president so they break off

00:03:59.439 --> 00:04:01.499
with the secure package.

00:04:01.499 --> 00:04:04.939
These last two cars are both filled with secret
service agents.

00:04:04.939 --> 00:04:07.019
The closer one is called the halfback.

00:04:07.019 --> 00:04:10.159
It carries the president’s personal secret
service detail.

00:04:10.159 --> 00:04:13.389
These are the agents that are on the president’s
person at all times.

00:04:13.389 --> 00:04:16.480
The second one is the CAT car—the Counter-Assault
Team.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:20.470
You can always identify these cars since they’re
the ones with the back window open.

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:24.790
That’s because the agents are sitting in
rear-facing seats with automatic weapons,

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:26.850
ready to respond to threats.

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:30.910
While its hard to see, you can just make out
the counter-assault team in this car in black

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:35.290
battle dress uniforms and the president’s
secret service detail in this car in coat

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:36.290
and tie.

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:40.390
In the event of an attack, the president’s
secret service detail is in charge of shielding

00:04:40.390 --> 00:04:45.850
and evacuating the president while the counter-assault
team would generally stay and repel the attack.

00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:50.700
These vans following the secure package are
full of the press pool reporting on the president.

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:55.450
This car is called the ID car and the secret
service agents inside are the ones that communicate

00:04:55.450 --> 00:05:00.890
with outside counter-surveillance teams that
proactively identify any threat to the motorcade.

00:05:00.890 --> 00:05:04.430
This black truck is the Hazardous Materials
Mitigation Unit.

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:10.160
It has equipment and personnel who can identify
and respond to chemical, nuclear, or biological

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:12.270
attacks on the motorcade.

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:14.920
Continuing on, you can see more press vans.

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:19.030
These motorcycles are speeding by the side
because, now that the motorcade has passed

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:22.450
the exit that they were blocking off, they
are going to the front of the motorcade to

00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:24.220
block off more exits.

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:25.970
This car is called the roadrunner.

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:30.250
It has a number of antennas on top to act
as a mobile communications center.

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:34.970
It connects the motorcade to the outside world
through satellite and internet communications

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:36.080
systems.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:40.400
Following this is a generic ambulance in case
of injury to any person in the motorcade.

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:43.860
Like the police officers at the front of the
motorcade, this ambulance just comes from

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:47.440
the local fire department of wherever the
president is.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:52.140
This last secret service car is the support
car which carries more secret service agents

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:54.220
and essential staff members.

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:58.200
Lastly, these three police cars are there
to make sure that, now that the exits are

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:01.900
open again, no car speeds up and enters the
motorcade from behind.

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:05.910
It’s important to note that this is just
the standard motorcade configuration.

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:08.270
It often changes based on the environment.

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:14.100
When President Obama visited Iraq in 2009,
for example, his motorcade looked like this.

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:18.560
Military humvees led a convoy of identical
suburbans—one of which carried the president.

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:23.550
On top of the cars were more powerful versions
of the anti-RPG antennas found on the normal

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:26.120
Electronic Countermeasures Suburban.

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:28.220
So, motorcades are incredibly efficient.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:32.240
It’s been reported that motorcades have
made the trip between Laguardia airport and

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:35.420
the Waldorf Astoria Hotel in Manhattan in
8-10 minutes.

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:40.520
That’s a 10 mile drive that takes a normal
driver 25-30 minutes.

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:46.190
Although, the safest motorcade is still no
motorcade since its the most exposed the president

00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:51.250
ever is so the secret service will typically
only use motorcades for trips up to 30 minutes

00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:52.380
long.

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:57.470
Motorcades are also incredibly logistically
difficult and expensive, so whenever possible,

00:06:57.470 --> 00:06:59.820
the president will travel on Marine One.

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:04.090
Marine one isn’t actually the name of a
specific helicopter, it’s rather the callsign

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:07.190
used by any helicopter with the president
onboard.

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:12.510
The Marine Corps operates 35 total helicopters
of a few different types as Marine One’s

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:16.330
which might seem like overkill, but these
helicopters are often also used to transport

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:20.860
the Vice President—when they’re known
as Marine Two—or government VIP’s.

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:24.830
While Marine One is occasionally used to transport
the president from point to point, its most

00:07:24.830 --> 00:07:28.450
frequent mission is to bring the president
from the White House to Andrews Air Force

00:07:28.450 --> 00:07:29.450
Base.

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:33.570
The presidents planes are based at Andrews
Air Force Base so this 12 mile trip is how

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:37.290
the president starts and finishes any long-distance
trip.

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:41.210
Much like the president’s limo, Marine One
never flies alone.

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:46.270
Right after take off, its met by as many as
five other identical helicopters which shuffle

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:49.870
positions in flight to throw off potential
attackers.

00:07:49.870 --> 00:07:55.290
Air Force One is also not a particular aircraft
but rather the callsign used whenever the

00:07:55.290 --> 00:07:57.380
president is aboard an Air Force aircraft.

00:07:57.380 --> 00:08:02.330
Typically, the role is served by one of two
modified Boeing 747’s.

00:08:02.330 --> 00:08:06.890
To give you a sense of how efficient this
entire transport system is, let’s time a

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:10.210
trip from the White house to UN Headquarters
in New York.

00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:15.630
The 12 mile flight to Andrews Air Force Base
takes about 8 minutes on Marine One, then,

00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:18.960
upon arrival, the president steps onboard
Air Force One.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:23.800
The president is always the last person to
step on Air Force One, so the second he’s

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:27.320
there the door closes and the aircraft taxies
for takeoff.

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:31.620
That means there’s about 5 minutes between
touchdown of Marine One and take off of Air

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:32.800
Force One.

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:37.620
From there it’s about a 30 minute flight
to JFK airport in New York followed by a 7

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:43.389
minute trip on Marine One to Downtown Manhattan
Heliport, then a 6 minute motorcade up FDR

00:08:43.389 --> 00:08:44.819
Drive to the United Nations.

00:08:44.819 --> 00:08:49.769
That means that, if everything goes perfectly,
including transfer times, it will take almost

00:08:49.769 --> 00:08:54.860
exactly one hour for the president to go from
stepping out of the White House to stepping

00:08:54.860 --> 00:08:56.790
inside the United Nations.

00:08:56.790 --> 00:08:58.870
And what is the cost of this hour long trip?

00:08:58.870 --> 00:09:06.570
Well its public information that Air Force
One costs an estimated $206,337 per flight

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:14.050
hour, so this half hour flight costs $103,169,
but of course we need to include Marine One.

00:09:14.050 --> 00:09:17.819
Marine One by itself is actually fairly inexpensive
to operate.

00:09:17.819 --> 00:09:21.990
While we don’t know its exact operating
cost, one of the helicopters often used as

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:28.850
Marine One is the Sikorsky UH-60 Black Hawk
which costs $2,199 per flight hour.

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:34.259
But of course Marine One always flies in formation
with up to 5 other helicopters.

00:09:34.259 --> 00:09:42.440
Per helicopter, a quarter hour of flight time
costs $530 or $3,180 total.

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:45.830
The motorcade’s cost for the short trip
is fairly minuscule, so we’ll leave it out

00:09:45.830 --> 00:09:47.029
for simplicity.

00:09:47.029 --> 00:09:51.089
But the helicopters and cars need to get to
New York, and this is done by loading them

00:09:51.089 --> 00:10:00.889
up on a C-5 Galaxy cargo plane which costs
$100,941 per flight hour or $50,471 for the

00:10:00.889 --> 00:10:03.170
half hour trip to New York.

00:10:03.170 --> 00:10:11.660
Altogether, that’s $156,820 for the hour
long trip to New York or $2,614 per minute,

00:10:11.660 --> 00:10:14.649
and keep in mind the president needs to travel
back too.

00:10:14.649 --> 00:10:20.360
Ultimately, transportation of the President
costs the American taxpayer about $350,000,000

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:25.050
per year—roughly 1/4 of the $1.4 billion
yearly white house budget.

00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:29.149
This is certainly a steep price, but many
would argue that its a small price to pay

00:10:29.149 --> 00:10:32.720
to keep the most powerful person in the world
safe.

